{
  "summary": "This experiment replaced the PCA-2000 linear encoder with the learned scvi_n200 nonlinear encoder while holding all other design variables constant (single profile, RNA-only, no aggregation, no fusion), yielding a validation accuracy of 0.520 and F1 of 0.498 versus the PCA baseline of 0.485 and 0.464. Because exactly one variable (the RNA encoder) was changed, the ~3.5-point accuracy gain is cleanly attributable to the encoder choice, indicating that nonlinear learned representations provide a modest but real improvement over linear projection in the RNA-only setting. However, absolute performance remains moderate, leaving it uncertain whether the RNA signal itself is the primary bottleneck and whether the scvi encoder's advantage will persist when combined with multiprofile aggregation or image-based multimodal fusion."
}

